WayHop low-memory patch for amneziawg-go v3.1.20260828 (b5928efb6ca19f0153958460c3d141f04abc5c2e).

Turns three link-time/compile-time settings into values read once at startup from the environment.
With none of the variables set the binary behaves exactly as upstream's.

  AWG_GO_BUFFERS_PER_POOL  cap of every buffer pool (upstream PreallocatedBuffersPerPool, 0 on
                           Linux = unbounded). 0 keeps upstream's unbounded pools; 1..511 is raised
                           to 512, because the TUN reader and the IPv4 and IPv6 socket readers each
                           hold a whole batch of 128 buffers for as long as they run and a cap must
                           leave a batch in flight beyond those. A full pool blocks the reader that
                           asks (backpressure), as upstream's Android and iOS builds already do.
  AWG_GO_QUEUE_SIZE        length of the inbound, outbound and handshake queues (upstream 1024),
                           clamped to 16..65536.
  AWG_SOCKET_DIR           directory of the UAPI sockets, overriding the link-time default
                           (WayHop links /tmp/wayhop/amneziawg); absolute paths only.

Values that do not parse are ignored and named. When any variable is set, the process that runs the
device logs one INFO line with the effective values (unless LOG_LEVEL=silent).

Applied by scripts/build-amneziawg-go.sh after the pinned-commit check, with `git apply`; its sha256
is recorded in the build's MANIFEST. Upstream's own device tests pass with it (TestAWGDevicePing is
flaky upstream as well, unpatched: 4 of 6 runs failed on the pinned commit).

